Here is a quick-reference summary of the key concepts covered in your Day 28 reading on APIs and HTTP:Key Takeaways: API & HTTP Basics1. What is an API?Definition: Application Programming Interface — a structured set of rules and protocols that allows different software applications to communicate with each other.Web APIs: Interface between a service provider and external applications using web protocols (primarily standard HTTP).Data Formats: Responses are typically returned in structured formats, most commonly JSON or XML.REST (Representational State Transfer): The dominant architectural style for modern web APIs, shifting away from older, heavier protocols like SOAP.2. The HTTP Request–Response CycleHTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol) operates on a Client-Server model:Client (e.g., a browser or mobile app) sends an HTTP Request.Server processes the request and sends back an HTTP Response.Connection completes or terminates.Structure of HTTP MessagesBoth Request and Response messages contain four core parts:Initial Line (Status Line)Zero or more Header Lines (Metadata)A Blank Line (Delimiter)Optional Message Body (Payload)3. HTTP Request Methods vs. CRUD OperationsRESTful APIs map standard HTTP methods directly to database operations (CRUD):HTTP MethodCRUD OperationDescriptionGETReadRetrieves data from the server without modifying anything.POSTCreateSends new data to the server to create a new resource.PUTUpdateReplaces/updates existing target resources with new data.DELETEDeleteRemoves the specified resource from the server.4. HTTP Headers & Status CodesCommon Headers:Content-Type: Tells the receiver what data format is in the body (e.g., application/json, text/html).Content-Length: Indicates the size of the message body in bytes.User-Agent: Identifies the client/browser making the request.Key Status Codes:200 OK: Request succeeded; data returned successfully.201 Created: Resource successfully created (often after a POST).400 Bad Request: Client sent malformed or invalid data.404 Not Found: The requested endpoint or resource does not exist.500 Internal Server Error: Something went wrong on the server side.